{
"claims": [
  {
    "claim_text": "In March 2026 a SQLite developer found and fixed a rare corruption bug known as the WAL-reset bug.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [0] states: 'On 2026-03-03, one of the SQLite developers (Dan) found and fixed a bug... We call this the \"WAL-reset bug\".' Passage [3] describes it as unlikely to occur in common use.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "quote": "On 2026-03-03, one of the SQLite developers (Dan) found and fixed a bug that could, in rare cases, lead to database corruption. We call this the \"WAL-reset bug\"."
      },
      {
        "index": 3,
        "quote": "It is unlikely to occur in common use."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "It is likely present in every release from 3.7.0 through 3.51.2 and is fixed in 3.51.3 and later, with backports available for 3.44.6 and 3.50.6.",
    "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
    "reasoning": "While the version range and fix version are correct per Passage [1], the claim states backports are available for 3.50.6. Passage [1] explicitly states backports are available for '3.44.6 and 3.50.7'.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 1,
        "quote": "The bug is likely present in all version of SQLite from 3.7.0 (2010-07-21) through 3.51.2 (2026-01-09). It is fixed in version 3.51.3 (2026-03-13) and later. Backports of the fix are available for some earlier releases: 3.44.6 and 3.50.7."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "The bug can only occur when two or more connections in separate threads or processes try to write or checkpoint at the same instant.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [2] states: 'The bug only affects databases in WAL mode when there are two or more database connections open on the same file, in separate threads or processes, and when those two connections attempt to write or checkpoint at the same instant.'",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 2,
        "quote": "The bug only affects databases in WAL mode when there are two or more database connections open on the same file, in separate threads or processes, and when those two connections attempt to write or checkpoint at the same instant."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "The bug affects rollback-journal databases as well as WAL databases.",
    "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [2] explicitly limits the bug's effect to 'databases in WAL mode'. The answer incorrectly claims it also affects rollback-journal databases.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 2,
        "quote": "The bug only affects databases in WAL mode..."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "The developers reproduced it organically in the lab before writing the fix.",
    "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [3] and Passage [8] state the opposite: 'The developers were unable to reproduce the bug organically' and 'The only way the developers have been able to cause the malfunction was to modify the SQLite sources... Without that code hack, the problem has never been observed during development and testing.'",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 3,
        "quote": "The developers were unable to reproduce the bug organically and had to add special testing logic to SQLite that deliberately triggers the circumstances of the the bug in order to verify that the issue has been fixed."
      },
      {
        "index": 8,
        "quote": "So much so that the SQLite developers were unable to reproduce the bug organically in the lab. The only way the developers have been able to cause the malfunction was to modify the SQLite sources to invoke a callback controlled by sqlite3_test_control() that enables a test program or script to trigger the write transaction of step 3 at just the right moment during the second checkpoint. Without that code hack, the problem has never been observed during development and testing."
      }
